  <prompt>Expose each container's URL in the Containers grid and detail — it is the scoping key for a Purview eDiscovery search and is surfaced nowhere today. Add in-app guidance that SPE compliance is governed in Purview, with a deep-link. An admin looking for "legal hold" should be routed, not stonewalled.</prompt>

  <role>SPAARKE platform developer. Expert in the Graph SPE container model and Fluent UI v9. Follow ADRs strictly.</role>

  <goal>Container URL is visible and copyable per container; Purview guidance with a working deep-link is present on a relevant surface.</goal>

  <context>
    <background>Spec FR-C10 + FR-C11 / §4.2c. SPE compliance is delivered through Microsoft Purview, not container-level app APIs. Building hold/retention management here would duplicate an audited compliance surface with a narrower, unauditable one — so R2 routes admins out instead. The container URL is the concrete thing an admin needs to scope an eDiscovery search under the SharePoint sites workload.</background>
    <relevant-files>
      <file role="modify">src/server/api/Sprk.Bff.Api/Infrastructure/Graph/SpeAdminGraphService.cs</file>
      <file role="modify">src/server/api/Sprk.Bff.Api/Api/SpeAdmin/ContainerEndpoints.cs</file>
      <file role="modify">src/solutions/SpeAdminApp/src/components/containers</file>
    </relevant-files>
    <dependencies>
      <dependency task="020" status="pending">v1.0 endpoints — the URL field must be requested from the correct version.</dependency>
      <dependency task="040" status="pending">WireMock fixture for the $select assertion.</dependency>
    </dependencies>
  </context>

  <constraints>
    <constraint source="project">Scope: the URL field. Verify the actual property name on the v1.0 container entity before adding it to $select — this is the exact defect class task 022 fixed; do not repeat it.</constraint>
    <constraint source="project">Scope: compliance features. Do NOT build hold, retention, or eDiscovery management. R2 routes to Purview only (spec §4.2c). Adding even a read-only hold-state column requires a spike first and is out of scope here.</constraint>
    <constraint source="project">Scope: the deep-link. It MUST resolve to the Purview compliance portal. A dead link is worse than no link.</constraint>
    <constraint source="NFR-06">Scope: the URL column. If a container has no URL, show an explicit absent state — never a blank cell that reads as an empty string.</constraint>
    <constraint source="ADR-021">Scope: the grid and guidance UI. Fluent v9 semantic tokens; dark-mode correct.</constraint>
    <constraint source="CLAUDE.md §10">Scope: this BFF change. Placement Justification in the PR; verify publish size.</constraint>
  </constraints>

  <knowledge>
    <topic>SPE container entity fields + Purview compliance boundary</topic>
    <files>
      <file>.claude/adr/ADR-021-fluent-design-system.md</file>
      <file>.claude/adr/ADR-019-problemdetails.md</file>
      <file>.claude/constraints/api.md</file>
      <file>knowledge/sharepoint-embedded/docs/learn-containers.md</file>
      <file>projects/sdap-SPE-admin-app-r2/spec.md</file>
      <file>projects/sdap-SPE-admin-app-r2/notes/spe-platform-research-2026-08-20.md</file>
    </files>
    <patterns>
      <pattern name="graph-sdk-v5" location=".claude/patterns/auth/graph-sdk-v5.md">$select construction.</pattern>
      <pattern name="endpoint-definition" location=".claude/patterns/api/endpoint-definition.md">Minimal API endpoint shape.</pattern>
    </patterns>
  </knowledge>

  <steps mode="directional">
    <step order="0" name="Context + rigor declaration">Declare rigor FULL. Load spec §4.2c.</step>
    <step order="1">Verify the container URL property name on the v1.0 entity; add it to $select and the DTO.</step>
    <step order="2">Render it in the Containers grid and detail with a copy affordance.</step>
    <step order="3">Add Purview guidance text explaining that hold, retention, and eDiscovery are governed in Purview, with the container URL as the scoping key.</step>
    <step order="4">Add a deep-link to the Purview compliance portal and verify it resolves.</step>
    <step order="5">Add WireMock coverage asserting the URL field is requested.</step>
    <step order="6">Build BFF and code page; run tests.</step>
    <step order="7">Verify against Spaarke Dev: URLs render for real containers.</step>
    <step order="8">Measure publish size; record. Update TASK-INDEX.md: task 028 → ✅. Document deviations in notes/.</step>
  </steps>

  <escalation>
    <trigger>If the v1.0 container entity exposes no URL property, STOP and escalate — the eDiscovery scoping story depends on it, and synthesizing a URL from other fields would be a fabricated value, which NFR-06 forbids.</trigger>
  </escalation>

  <ui-tests>
    <test name="Container URL visible and copyable">
      <steps>
        <step>Open the Containers grid</step>
        <step>Locate the URL for a container and copy it</step>
      </steps>
      <expected>The URL renders and can be copied. A container without a URL shows an explicit absent state, not a blank cell.</expected>
    </test>
    <test name="Purview guidance routes the admin">
      <steps>
        <step>Look for hold/retention/eDiscovery in the app</step>
        <step>Follow the Purview deep-link</step>
      </steps>
      <expected>Guidance explains Purview governs these, names the container URL as the scoping key, and the deep-link resolves to the compliance portal.</expected>
    </test>
    <test name="Dark Mode Compliance">
      <steps><step>Toggle dark mode and inspect the grid and guidance</step></steps>
      <expected>Fluent v9 semantic tokens per ADR-021; no hex literals.</expected>
    </test>
  </ui-tests>

  <tools>
    <tool name="dotnet">Build and test the BFF</tool>
    <tool name="npm">Build the SpeAdminApp code page</tool>
  </tools>

  <outputs>
    <output type="code">src/server/api/Sprk.Bff.Api/Infrastructure/Graph/SpeAdminGraphService.cs</output>
    <output type="code">src/solutions/SpeAdminApp/src/components/containers</output>
  </outputs>

  <acceptance-criteria>
    <criterion testable="true">Container URL renders in the Containers grid and detail and can be copied.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Purview guidance is present and its deep-link resolves to the compliance portal.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">The URL property name is verified against the v1.0 entity before use.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Negative: a container without a URL shows an explicit absent state, not a blank cell (NFR-06).</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Negative: no hold, retention, or eDiscovery MANAGEMENT capability was built (spec §4.2c boundary).</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">dotnet build 0 errors; code page builds; tests green; publish size within ceiling.</criterion>
  </acceptance-criteria>

  <notes>
    Spec FR-C10 + FR-C11. Satisfies spec success criterion 10.

    COMPLETED 2026-08-24. Full record: notes/task-028-findings.md

    🔴 The constraint to verify the property name found something sharper than a wrong name:
    `fileStorageContainer` has NO URL property in EITHER API version. The URL lives on the `drive`
    navigation property. And on the containers COLLECTION Graph accepts
    `$expand=drive($select=webUrl)`, returns 200, echoes `drive(webUrl)` in its own `@odata.context`
    — and omits `drive` from every row. Both versions, every expand shape tried. The natural
    implementation would have shipped an empty URL column backed by a 200 and a context header
    asserting the field was present: this project's signature defect, arriving from the platform.
    GET-single DOES return it, and `$select` does NOT suppress it there (verified with the code's
    actual 7-field $select, not a simplified probe — having just watched $select co-occur with a
    dropped expand, that could not be assumed).

    ESCALATION TRIGGER evaluated, did NOT fire. Its antecedent is literally true (v1.0 exposes no URL
    property) but its purpose is satisfied: the URL is obtainable from Graph as a real value. The
    fabrication path was live and was refused — the container id decodes to the SharePoint site GUID
    that appears in the URL, so a URL could be assembled with no Graph call, but the tenant hostname
    is nowhere in the id, so any assembled URL hard-codes a guess. Exactly what NFR-06 forbids.

    ⚠️ DEVIATION from AC-1's literal wording: the grid resolves the URL per row on demand rather than
    eagerly, because per the above an eager column is not purchasable short of one Graph call per row
    per grid load. Detail carries it unconditionally at zero extra cost. The <goal> — "visible and
    copyable per container" — is met in full; see findings §3.

    Wire contract: list rows omit the `webUrl` KEY entirely (JsonIgnore WhenWritingNull) so absence
    cannot be misread as "this container has no URL".

    Purview link is the portal ROOT, deliberately: a bogus path returns the same 302 as /ediscovery
    (auth-gated SPA), so a 302 proves the host, never the path. Navigation is in the guidance text.

    🔴 Fixed in passing — the review harness was a source of the fabricated data the operator
    reported: `createdDateTime` was invented from container NAMES (Spaarke Dev Container 2 said
    2025-09-30; truth is 2026-05-28, matching the operator's M365 screenshot), and
    `GET /spe/containers/{id}` returned CONTAINERS[0] for EVERY id — which would have hidden this
    task entirely by resolving every row's URL to the same container's.
  </notes>

  <execution><skill>.claude/skills/task-execute/SKILL.md</skill></execution>
</task>
